    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1295">

                <pb facs="1250" n="964" xml:id="pg964"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/2643</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Germany</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gerard</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-06-17T14:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 17, 1916, 2 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3101. Your 4001, June 15, 2 p. m. In order to make position of Department
                    definite, it would be better if it could state that Doctor Taylor’s report was
                    published with consent of German authorities. Ascertain whether German
                    authorities will give such consent and report earliest possible moment.<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d1295-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1295-f1" n="1" xml:id="d1295-f1-content">
                <p>In his telegram No. 4050, June 26, 1916 [received June 27], the Ambassador
                    stated: “German Government has no objection to publication Doctor Taylor’s milk
                    report.” It was accordingly given to the press and printed for distribution to
                    inquirers on the subject.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
